# Build output
build/
*.o
*.elf
*.iso

# The 2011 Windows toolchain (55 MB of binaries, kept locally under
# legacy/tools/). The batch files next to it ARE tracked -- they are small and
# they document how the project used to be built.
legacy/tools/

# NOT a bare "tools/". That pattern has no slash in front of it, so git matches
# it at EVERY depth, and the top level tools/ directory -- which holds the
# scripts the Makefile invokes -- became invisible. Nothing errors when that
# happens: "git add" says nothing, "git status" shows a clean tree, and the
# file is simply not in the commit. It cost one script already, and the way it
# announced itself was "git rm" reporting that the file it was looking at
# matched nothing.

# Running tools/smoke-test.py leaves one of these next to the script.
__pycache__/

# Editor and OS droppings
.DS_Store
*~
*.swp
